Orange Pi 4 Pro headless kernel experiment
=========================================

Base: official Linux 6.18.62 LTS. Board support: the pinned Xunlong source
listed in sources.json. Git history separates the unchanged source import
from 6.18 interface adaptations. Original notices are preserved. This is
not an official Orange Pi image or a claim of upstream board support.

The initial target is a wired Debian server: SD, NVMe root, Ethernet,
serial console, thermal protection, cpufreq, regulators and PSCI CPU idle.
GPU, NPU, wireless, display, sound and board USB support are not qualified
and are disabled in the initial configuration. Other kernel series will
use separate branches; no 7.x branch is qualified yet.

On an Ubuntu 24.04 amd64 build host, install:
  sudo apt-get install build-essential gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu \
    binutils-aarch64-linux-gnu bc bison flex libssl-dev libelf-dev \
    dwarves python3 python3-dev rsync device-tree-compiler u-boot-tools

Then, from this checkout:
  O=/absolute/path/to/build JOBS=8 scripts/orangepi/build.sh

Use a fresh output directory for an independent build. The script resolves
the versioned board defconfig, verifies required built-in drivers, fixes
build identity/timestamp, and builds Image, modules and the board DTB.
Module link failures are fatal. A clean checkout is required; the source commit
is embedded in the kernel release so different commits use separate module
directories. The compiler and other host packages are
recorded for the initial experiment; the host environment is not yet pinned
in a container and byte-for-byte reproducibility has not been established.

Outputs are under the chosen build directory:
  arch/arm64/boot/Image
  arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-orangepi-4-pro.dtb
  vmlinux, System.map, Module.symvers, .config and built modules

Board qualification restrictions
-------------------------------

The headless DT disables the optional eMMC controller: its empty-slot probe
blocks boot in the initial 6.18 port. This is an isolation workaround, not an
eMMC driver fix. eMMC modules are not supported by this configuration yet.
SD remains the firmware/boot medium and NVMe is the target root filesystem.

The upstream Motorcomm PHY driver needs explicit board data that the vendor
driver supplied or preserved implicitly: 125 MHz clock output and RGMII PHY
delays of 1900 ps RX / 150 ps TX. The existing MAC delay taps are retained.
The PHY values reproduce the working vendor setup; no PHY register writes
are added to the driver.

Status: a full cross-build passed on Ubuntu 24.04. Early hardware tests reached
Debian 13 on NVMe and wired SSH with these board settings applied in RAM.
A build and boot of this tree's DTB must still be checked separately. Original
vendor firmware and a known-good kernel are retained for supervised fallback.
This is not production qualification: storage integrity, link recovery,
thermal/DVFS behavior and idle power require runtime testing. Debian packaging
and weekly CI remain follow-up work; this branch does not implement unattended
updates or boot selection. Hardware trials use an external supervisor, not a
qualified native boot watchdog.
